# Tutorial 09 — Puppeteer Timeline
# How to create complex choreographed animations using timeline blocks in VNS.

@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_09_puppeteer
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
{{BG_START}}[inc tutorial_count]
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[show lavender center happy]
[wait 200]
Lavender: Ready for the power tools?
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The Puppeteer timeline lets you choreograph multiple characters, camera moves, and effects in a single synchronized block.
Lavender: Think of it like a film director calling "action" — everything moves at once.

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: What would you like to learn about?
> What is a timeline block? -> section_what
> Move tracks -> section_move_tracks
> Event cues -> section_events
> Camera tracks -> section_camera
> Editor workflow -> section_editor
> Quick reference card -> reference_sheet
> Practice scene -> practice_scene
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── What is a Timeline Block ────────────────────────────────────

@label section_what
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: A {b}timeline{/b} block runs multiple animation tracks in parallel — moves, rotations, events, camera shifts — all synchronized to the same clock.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}timeline {{}}{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}  move "lavender" {{}} from left to center easing easeInOut duration 600 {{}}{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}  move "alex" {{}} from right to center easing easeOut duration 500 {{}}{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}{{}}{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Both characters move at the same time. No need to chain [wait] commands — the timeline handles it.
Lavender: Timelines are great for cutscenes, dramatic entrances, and any moment where multiple things happen simultaneously.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Think of each track as one instrument in an orchestra — the timeline is the conductor keeping them in sync.
> Next: Move tracks -> section_move_tracks
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Move Tracks ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_move_tracks
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: {b}Move tracks{/b} are the most common type. They slide a character from one position to another.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}move "lavender" {{}} from far_left to center easing easeInOut duration 600 {{}}{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: You specify: who, where from, where to, which easing curve, and how long.
Lavender: You can also add keyframes at specific times for complex paths:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}move "lavender" {{}}{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}  0ms: at 0.1,0.8{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}  300ms: at 0.5,0.6{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}  600ms: at 0.9,0.8{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}{{}}{/color}
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: That creates an arc — the character moves up to the center, then back down to the right.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: For simple A-to-B moves, stick with from/to. For anything more complex, use keyframes.
> Next: Event cues -> section_events
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Event Cues ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_events
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: {b}Event tracks{/b} fire at specific moments during the timeline — expression changes, sound effects, screen flashes.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}event "sfx" {{}} at 200ms: "door_slam.wav" {{}}{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}event "expression" {{}} at 400ms: "lavender" "surprised" {{}}{/color}
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Events don't animate over time — they trigger instantly at their timestamp.
Lavender: Use them to sync audio, expressions, and screen effects precisely with character movement.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: A well-timed expression change during a move can make a scene feel alive. The character looks surprised {i}exactly{/i} when they stop moving.
> Next: Camera -> section_camera
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Camera Tracks ───────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_camera
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: {b}Camera tracks{/b} pan and zoom the view during the timeline.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}camera {{}} pan from 0,0 to 100,0 easing easeInOut duration 800 {{}}{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}camera {{}} zoom from 1.0 to 1.3 easing easeIn duration 600 {{}}{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Camera movement combined with character movement creates cinematic sequences — think of a tracking shot following a character across a room.
Lavender: Zoom in on a character's face during an emotional moment, or pan slowly to reveal a new part of the scene.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Subtle camera work goes a long way. Even a tiny 5% zoom during dialogue creates a sense of focus and intimacy.
> Next: Editor workflow -> section_editor
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Editor Workflow ─────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_editor
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: The Puppeteer is a visual timeline editor in the JVN Editor. You can build animations by dragging keyframes, then export them to VNS code.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The workflow:
narrator: {b}1.{/b} Open the Puppeteer from the editor toolbar.
narrator: {b}2.{/b} Add characters and set up your scene.
narrator: {b}3.{/b} Drag keyframes on the timeline to position characters at specific moments.
narrator: {b}4.{/b} Preview the animation in real time.
narrator: {b}5.{/b} Export to VNS — the editor generates the timeline block for you.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: You can also import existing VNS timeline blocks back into the Puppeteer, edit them visually, and re-export.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: The round-trip means you're never stuck — visual or code, whichever works best for the moment.
> Reference card -> reference_sheet
> Try it yourself -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Reference Card ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label reference_sheet
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Puppeteer timeline cheat sheet:
narrator: {b}timeline {{}} ... {{}}{/b} — define a synchronized animation block.
narrator: {b}move "id" {{}} from pos to pos easing E duration N {{}}{/b} — character movement track.
narrator: {b}event "type" {{}} at Nms: data {{}}{/b} — trigger a point event (sfx, expression, etc).
narrator: {b}camera {{}} pan/zoom from X to Y easing E duration N {{}}{/b} — camera track.
narrator: {b}Keyframe syntax:{/b} Nms: value — place values at specific timestamps.
narrator: {b}Editor:{/b} Puppeteer tool builds timelines visually, exports to VNS.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Timelines are the most powerful VNS feature. Start simple and build complexity as you get comfortable.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice Scene ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Let's imagine some timeline scenarios. Pick one:
> Two characters meet in the middle -> practice_meet
> A dramatic zoom-in -> practice_zoom
> A character enters with fanfare -> practice_fanfare

@label practice_meet
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: Imagine both of us walking toward each other from opposite sides...
[move lavender center easeInOut 600]
[wait 600]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: In a real timeline, both characters would move simultaneously. The timeline handles the sync — you just set the keyframes.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Two move tracks, same duration, opposite directions. Simple and effective.
[set practice_result 1]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_zoom
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Picture this — the camera slowly pushes in while I'm speaking. The background blurs slightly. The focus narrows.
[show lavender center emphasis]
Lavender: That's what a camera zoom track does. A subtle shift from 1.0 to 1.2 over 800ms changes the entire mood.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: You can pair it with a move track too — zoom in while the character steps forward.
[set practice_result 2]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_fanfare
[hide lavender]
[wait 400]
narrator: A beat of silence. Then — the doors burst open.
[show lavender far_right happy]
[move lavender center easeOut 500]
[wait 500]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: In a full timeline, you'd add an SFX event at 0ms for the door sound, an expression change at 300ms, and maybe a camera shake at 100ms.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Layer events on top of movement — that's what makes timelines feel cinematic.
[set practice_result 3]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_wrap
[show lavender center happy]
[if practice_result == 1]
Lavender: Parallel movement is the bread and butter of timelines. Master this and everything else builds on top.
[elif practice_result == 2]
Lavender: Camera work is subtle but transformative. A tiny zoom changes how the player reads the entire scene.
[else]
Lavender: Layered events turn simple movement into storytelling. Each track adds another dimension.
[endif]
Lavender: Open the Puppeteer in the editor and try building one of these — it's much easier than writing the code by hand.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

@label return_hub
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_2]
